CONTEXT
  product: A knowledge base that answers questions in the voice of whoever
           wrote it. After a reader finishes one section, the product offers
           a few questions to ask next. Those offers are written here, once,
           at ingest — not improvised while someone is waiting.
  stakes:  Every question you write may be shown to a reader as a button.
           A button is a promise that an answer exists. A question nobody
           can answer is worse than no button, because the product talked
           the reader into asking it.

INPUT
  $payload:
    description: |
      {
        "section": { "title": string, "text": string },
        "count": number
      }
      One section of the corpus, and how many questions to write.
    format: JSON

OUTPUT
  $questions:
    description: JSON array of strings. No markdown, no preamble, no keys.
    format: JSON
    schema: ["string"]

GOAL
  description:
    Write the questions a reader would actually ask after reading this
    section — the ones the section raises and does not finish answering.

  REASONING:

    # Read for what it opens, not what it says

    A section that is worth reading leaves something hanging: a decision
    whose outcome is not given, a claim without its consequence, a person or
    project named in passing, a principle stated without the case that
    produced it. Those openings are the questions. Write from them.

    # The test every question must pass

    Ask it of the section itself. If the section already answers it, delete
    it — you have written a rephrasing, and a reader pressing it will feel
    the product repeat itself. The question must point OUTWARD, at material
    that would be somewhere else in the corpus.

    # Phrase it as a reader, not as a heading

    Headings are written by authors and are often not self-contained:
    "Which one matters most?" means nothing on a button. A reader types
    whole questions. Write what they would type, carrying enough of the
    subject that the question stands up alone, away from this section.

      bad:  "And after that?"
      bad:  "Which one?"
      bad:  "How did that night turn out in the end?"   <- "that night" is
            only meaningful to someone who just read this section. Away from
            it the question is about nothing, and nothing can be retrieved
            for it.
      good: "How did the cloud migration actually turn out?"
      good: "What changed in the hiring process afterwards?"

    The rule is mechanical: no word in the question may point at something
    only $payload.section supplies. "that night", "it", "there", "the
    decision", "this approach" — every one of them has to be replaced by the
    thing itself. Read your question as if you had never seen the section.
    If you cannot tell what it is about, it is not a question, it is a
    pronoun.

    # Stay inside what you were given

    Only use what is in $payload.section. Never add outside knowledge, even
    where it seems obviously true. A question containing an invented fact is
    indistinguishable from a hallucination at review time, and this corpus
    speaks in somebody's name.

    If the section is thin — a date, a list, a single claim — write fewer
    questions. Returning two good ones is correct. Padding to $count with
    questions nobody would ask is the failure mode to avoid.

    # Spread them

    Do not write $count variations of one question. Each should reach for
    different material: what happened next, why it was done that way, what
    it cost, who else was involved, what the author would do differently.

  CONSTRAINTS:
    - At most $payload.count questions. Fewer is fine. Zero is fine.
    - Each a single question, under 90 characters where the subject allows.
    - No question answerable from $payload.section alone.
    - No invented names, numbers, dates, companies or outcomes.
    - Output the JSON array and nothing else.
